id: k_msgq_put
title: Send a message between threads
category: Message queues
version: 4.4.0
aliases: send queue message|message queue|send task message|enqueue data|post message|send to worker|interthread message
signature: int k_msgq_put(struct k_msgq *msgq, const void *data, k_timeout_t timeout);
source: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/v4.4.0/include/zephyr/kernel.h#L5342
related: K_MSGQ_DEFINE|k_msgq_get|k_msgq_init|k_msgq_purge
## Explanation
Copy one fixed-size message into a Zephyr message queue so another thread can receive it in FIFO order. The queue owns its copied message bytes, not your original pointer.
## Parameters
msgq: pointer to an initialized message queue.
data: pointer to an object whose size matches the queue's configured message size.
timeout: K_NO_WAIT, K_FOREVER, or a finite duration to wait for a free slot.
Returns: 0 on success, -ENOMSG if no wait and no space (or purged), or -EAGAIN after timeout.
## Example
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>
#include <stdint.h>

struct reading {
    uint32_t value;
};

K_MSGQ_DEFINE(reading_queue, sizeof(struct reading), 4, 4);

static void receiver(void *a, void *b, void *c)
{
    ARG_UNUSED(a);
    ARG_UNUSED(b);
    ARG_UNUSED(c);
    struct reading item;
    while (1) {
        if (k_msgq_get(&reading_queue, &item, K_FOREVER) == 0) {
            printk("Received: %u\n", (unsigned int)item.value);
        }
    }
}

K_THREAD_DEFINE(receiver_id, 1024, receiver, NULL, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, 0);

int main(void)
{
    uint32_t count = 0;
    while (1) {
        struct reading item = {.value = count++};
        if (k_msgq_put(&reading_queue, &item, K_NO_WAIT) != 0) {
            printk("Queue full: item not sent\n");
        }
        k_sleep(K_SECONDS(1));
    }
    return 0;
}
## Requirements
Assumptions: a kernel-thread-enabled Zephyr application and enough RAM for a four-element queue and receiver stack. K_MSGQ_DEFINE allocates a queue with fixed-size copied messages; here the alignment argument is 4. No peripheral or Devicetree configuration required.
For printed output, enable CONFIG_PRINTK and a suitable console in the project/board configuration if they are not already enabled.
## Mistakes
Do not enqueue a pointer when you meant to copy the pointed-to data: the queue copies exactly its configured message size. Check queue-full/timeout return codes. k_msgq_put() can be called from an ISR only with K_NO_WAIT, because blocking is not allowed. k_msgq_get() also requires K_NO_WAIT in an ISR.
